I think Andrew should have been stronger with his words. In pseudo-random
number generation, each integer is guaranteed to come up once in every
MAX_RAND turns, where MAX_RAND is the size of the pseudo-random number
generation pool. Your comment about "unlikely" is not appropriate here,
because the result could be determined, since pseudo-random sequences are
deterministic. I believe someone said that the number cycle has a period of
10^10, or in that region. This means that there will be no probabilities
below 0.0000000001, for starters, and it also means that every number in
that period will be visited over 10^10 calls to the RNG.

Therefore, there is no element of chance in this problem, just the
appearance of chance, and the decision on whether or not this is a viable
means of indefinite survival could be determined as true or false, given
someone took the time to figure out the specifics.

